== Chapter IX

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[Laying open the signs, or fruits, of love to God.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

#emph[Use] 2. Let us test ourselves impartially#footnote[#emph[Use] 2. Trial.] to see whether we are among those who love God. To settle this (for our love will best be seen by its fruits), I will set down fourteen signs, or fruits, of love to God. It concerns us closely to search whether any of these fruits grow in our garden.

1\. The first fruit of love is #emph[the mind’s musing on God.] The thoughts of a man who is in love#footnote[1\. #emph[Fruit] of love.] are always on the one he loves. He who loves God is ravished and carried away with the contemplation of God (Ps 139:18): “When I awake, I am still with You.” Thoughts are like travelers in the mind. #emph[David’s] thoughts kept to the road to heaven: “I am still with You.” God is the treasure, and where the treasure is, there the heart is. By this we may test our love to God. What are our thoughts most on? Can we say we are ravished with delight when we think of God? Have our thoughts got wings? Have they flown on high? Do we contemplate Christ and glory? Oh, how far are they from being lovers of God who hardly ever think of God! Ps 10:4: “In all his thoughts there is no God.” A sinner crowds God out of his thoughts. He never thinks of God except with horror, as the prisoner thinks of the judge.

2\. The second fruit of love is #emph[desire for communion.]#footnote[2\. #emph[Fruit.]] Love desires closeness and fellowship (Ps 84:2): “My heart and my flesh cry out for the living God.” King #emph[David] was shut out from the house of God, where the tabernacle was (the visible token of his presence). Now he breathes after God, and in a holy #emph[passion] of desire cries out for the living God. Lovers want to be talking together. If we love God, we prize his #emph[ordinances,] because there we meet with God. He speaks to us in his word, and we speak to him in prayer. By this let us examine our love to God. Do we desire close communion with God? Lovers cannot be apart for long. Those who love God have a holy fondness; they do not know how to be away from him. They can do without anything but God’s presence. They can do without health and friends, they can do without a full table, but they cannot do without God. Ps 143:7: “Do not hide Your face from me, or I will be like those who descend to the Pit.” Lovers have their λειποθυμίαι, their fainting fits. #emph[David] was ready to faint away and die when he had no sight of God. Those who love God cannot be content with having ordinances unless they may enjoy God in them. That would be to lick the glass and not the honey.

What shall we say to those who can live their whole lives without God? They think God is the thing they can best spare. They complain that they lack health and trade, but not that they lack God. Wicked men are not acquainted with God, and how can they love when they are not acquainted? Indeed, what is worse, they do not desire to be acquainted with God (Job 21:14): “They say to God: ‘Leave us alone! For we have no desire to know Your ways.’” Sinners shun acquaintance with God; they count his presence a burden. And are these lovers of God? Does that woman love her husband who cannot bear to be in his presence?

3\. The third fruit of love is #emph[grief.] Where there is love to God,#footnote[3\. #emph[Fruit.]] there is grieving for our sins of unkindness against him. A child who loves his father cannot help weeping for offending him. The heart that burns with love melts in tears. “Oh, that I should abuse the love of so dear a Savior! Did not my Lord suffer enough on the cross? Must I make him suffer more? Shall I give him more #emph[gall and vinegar] to drink? How disloyal and unworthy I have been! How I have grieved his Spirit, trampled on his royal commands, and slighted his blood!” This opens a vein of godly sorrow and makes the heart bleed afresh. Matt 26:75: “Peter … went outside and wept bitterly.” #emph[Peter] thought how dearly Christ had loved him. He had been taken up onto the mount of #emph[transfiguration;] Christ had shown him the glory of heaven in a vision. Now that he should deny Christ after receiving such remarkable love from him broke his heart with grief: “he went outside and wept bitterly.”

By this let us test our love to God. Are our hearts spiritual stills, dropping the water of godly tears? Do we grieve for our unkindness against God, our abuse of mercy, our failure to use our talents? How far from loving God are those who sin daily and whose hearts never strike them! They have a sea of sin and not a drop of sorrow. They are so far from being troubled that they make merry with their sins (Jer 11:15): “When you do evil, then you rejoice.” O wretch, did Christ bleed for sin, and do you laugh at it? These are far from loving God. Does a man love his friend when he loves to do him an injury?

4\. Another fruit of love is #emph[magnanimity,] greatness of spirit.#footnote[4\. #emph[Fruit.]] Love is valiant; it turns cowardice into courage. Love will make a man venture on the greatest difficulties and dangers. #emph[Amor nescit difficultates] \[Love knows no difficulties\]. The timid hen will fly at a dog or a snake to defend her young. Love pours μεγαλόψυχον θυμὸν, a spirit of gallantry and fortitude, into a Christian. He who loves God will stand up in his cause and be an advocate for him (Acts 4:20): “We cannot stop speaking about what we have seen and heard.” He who is afraid to own Christ has only a little love for him. #emph[Nicodemus] came sneaking to Christ #emph[at night] (John 3:2); he was afraid of being seen with him in the daytime. “Love drives out fear.” As the sun drives away fogs and mists, so divine love in great measure drives out carnal fear. Does a man love God when he can hear his blessed truths spoken against and stay silent? He who loves his friend will stand up for him and defend him when he is reviled. Does Christ appear for us in heaven, and are we afraid to appear for him on earth? Love puts heart into a Christian. It fires his heart with zeal and steels it with courage.

5\. The fifth fruit of love is #emph[tender feeling.] If we love God,#footnote[5\. #emph[Fruit.]] our hearts ache for the dishonors wicked men do to God. We see not only the banks of #emph[religion] but of #emph[common decency] broken down, and a flood of wickedness coming in. We see God’s Sabbaths profaned, his oath violated, his name dishonored. If there is any love to God in us, we will take these things to heart. #emph[Lot] was “a righteous man distressed by the depraved conduct of the lawless” (2 Pet 2:7). He took the sins of #emph[Sodom] and made spears of them to pierce his own soul. How far from loving God are those who are not moved at all by his dishonor! If only they have peace and trade, they take nothing to heart. A man who is dead drunk never notices or feels it, though another man is bleeding to death beside him. So many, being drunk with the wine of prosperity, feel nothing when the honor of God is wounded and his truths lie bleeding. If men loved God, they would grieve to see his glory suffer and religion itself become a martyr.

6\. The sixth fruit of love is #emph[hatred of sin.]#footnote[6\. #emph[Fruit.]] Fire purges the dross from the metal; the fire of love purges out sin. Hos 14:8: “Ephraim shall say, ‘What have I to do anymore with idols?’” He who loves God will have nothing to do with sin, except to give battle to it. Sin is #emph[Dei-cidium] \[the killing of God\]; it strikes not only at God’s #emph[honor] but at his #emph[being.] Does a man love his prince when he shelters a traitor to the crown? Is he a friend to God who loves what God hates#footnote[#emph[Quomodo amas Deum, cum adhuc amas quod odit Deus?] Augustine.]? The love of God and the love of sin cannot live together. The affections cannot be carried toward two opposites at the same time. A man cannot love health and love poison too, and so one cannot love God and sin too. He who allows any secret sin in his heart is as far from loving God as heaven is from earth.

7\. Another fruit of love is #emph[crucifixion.]#footnote[7\. #emph[Fruit.]] He who loves God is dead to the world (Gal 6:14): “I have been crucified … to the world.” I am dead to its honors and pleasures. He who is in love with God is not much in love with anything else#footnote[#emph[Cui Christus dulcescit, mundus amarescit.] \[To him for whom Christ grows sweet, the world grows bitter.\] Bernard.]. His love to God moves swiftly, like the sun in the sky; but his love to the world is like the motion of the sun on the dial, which is very slow. The love of God and a violent love of the world cannot stand together#footnote[#emph[Fortis est ut mors amor; sicut mors violentèr separat animas a corpore, ita amor Dei violentèr segregat hominem a mundano amore.] \[Love is strong as death. As death violently separates souls from the body, so the love of God violently separates a man from worldly love.\] Isidore.] (1 John 2:15): “If anyone loves the world, the love of the Father is not in him.” Love to God swallows up all other love, as #emph[Moses’] rod swallowed up the #emph[Egyptian] rods. If a man could live in the sun, what a small point the whole earth would be! So when a man’s heart is raised above the world in wondering at God and loving him, how poor and slight these things below are! They seem as nothing in his eye. It was a sign that the first Christians loved God that their money did not lie near their hearts: they #emph[laid their money at the apostles’ feet] (Acts 4:35).

Test your love to God by this. What shall we think of those who never have enough of the world? They have the dry dropsy of covetousness, thirsting without end after riches (Amos 2:7): “who pant after the dust of the earth.” Never talk of your love to Christ, says #emph[Ignatius,] ὅταν τὸν κόσμον προτιμᾶτε, when you prefer the world to the #emph[pearl of great price.] And are there not many such, who prize their gold above God? If they have a south land, they do not care for the #emph[water of life.] They will sell Christ and a good conscience for money. Will God ever give heaven to those who value him so basely, and prefer glittering dust to the glorious Deity? What is there in the earth that we should set our hearts on it so? It is only that the devil makes us look at it through a multiplying glass. The world has no real worth in itself; it is only paint and counterfeit gold. The world has two breasts. The one breast is dry: it is #emph[vanity.] The other breast runs blood: it is #emph[vexation.]

8\. The next fruit of love is #emph[fear.]#footnote[8\. #emph[Fruit.]] In the godly, love and fear kiss each other. Two kinds of fear arise from love.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. A fear of #emph[displeasing.] The wife loves her husband, and so she will rather deny herself than displease him. The more we love God, the more afraid we are of grieving his Spirit (Gen 39:9): “How could I do such a great evil and sin against God?” When the Empress #emph[Eudoxia] threatened to banish #emph[Chrysostom,] he said, “Tell her I fear nothing but sin.” That is a blessed love which puts a Christian into a hot fit of #emph[zeal] and a cold fit of #emph[fear,] making him shake and tremble, so that he does not dare willingly to offend God.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. A fear #emph[mixed with jealousy] (1 Sam 4:13): Eli’s “heart trembled for the ark of God.” It is not said that his heart trembled for #emph[Hophni] and #emph[Phinehas,] his two sons, but for the #emph[ark,] because if the #emph[ark] were taken, then “the glory has departed.” He who loves God is full of fear that it may go badly with the church. He fears that profaneness (which is the plague of leprosy) may increase, that popery may get a footing, that God may depart from a people. The presence of God in his ordinances is the beauty and strength of a nation. The #emph[Trojans] had the image of #emph[Pallas,] and they believed that as long as that image was kept among them, they would never be conquered. As long as God’s presence is with a people, they are safe. But the soul that is on fire with love to God fears that the visible tokens of God’s presence may be removed.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Let us test our love to God by this touchstone. Many fear that peace and trade may go, but not that God and his gospel may go. Are these lovers of God? He who loves God is more afraid of losing spiritual blessings than earthly ones. If the #emph[Sun of righteousness] departs from our horizon, what can follow but darkness? What comfort can an organ or an anthem give if the gospel is gone? Is it not like the sound of a trumpet, or a volley of shots, at a funeral?]

9\. If we are lovers of God, #emph[we love what God loves.]#footnote[9\. #emph[Fruit.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. We love God’s word. #emph[David] valued the word above honey for its #emph[sweetness] (Ps 119:103), and above gold for its #emph[worth] (Ps 119:72). The lines of Scripture are richer than mines of gold. We may well love the word: it is the guiding star that directs us to heaven; it is the field in which the #emph[pearl] is hidden. The man who does not love the word, but thinks it too strict, and could wish any part of the Bible torn out (as that adulterer did the seventh commandment), does not have the least spark of love in his heart.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. We love God’s day. We do not only keep a Sabbath, but love a Sabbath. Isa 58:13: “If you call the Sabbath a delight.” The Sabbath is what keeps up the face of religion among us; this day must be set apart as glorious to the Lord. The house of God is βασιλικὴ, the palace of the great King. On the Sabbath God shows himself there through the lattice. If we love God, we prize his day above all other days. The whole week would be dark if it were not for this day. On this day manna falls double. Now, if ever, heaven’s gate stands open, and God comes down in a golden shower. On this blessed day the Sun of righteousness rises on the soul. How a gracious heart prizes that day, which was made for the very purpose of enjoying God!]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. We love God’s laws. A gracious soul is glad of the law, because it checks his sinful excesses. The heart would be ready to run wild in sin if the law of God did not put some blessed restraints on it. He who loves God loves his law: the law of repentance, the law of self-denial. Many say they love God, but they hate his laws (Ps 2:3): “Let us break Their chains and cast away Their cords.” God’s precepts are compared to #emph[cords;] they bind men to good behavior. But the wicked think these #emph[cords] too tight, and so they say, #emph[let us break them.] They profess to love Christ as a #emph[Savior,] but hate him as a #emph[Prince.] Christ tells us of his #emph[yoke] (Matt 11:29). Sinners would have Christ put a crown on their head, but not a yoke on their neck. He would be a strange king who ruled without laws.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. We love God’s picture; we love his image shining in the saints (1 John 5:1): “Everyone who loves the Father also loves those born of Him.” It is possible to love a saint and yet not love him #emph[as] a saint. We may love him for something else: for his good nature, or because he is friendly and generous. A beast loves a man, but not #emph[quatenus homo, as] he is a man; it loves him because he feeds it and gives it fodder. But to love a saint #emph[as he is a saint] is a sign of love to God. If we love a saint for his saintliness, as having something of God in him, then we love him in these four cases.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. We love a saint though he is poor. A man who loves gold loves a piece of gold though it is wrapped in a rag. So though a saint is in rags, we love him, because there is #emph[aliquid Christi,] something of Christ, in him.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. We love a saint though he has many personal failings. There is no perfection here. In some, rash anger prevails; in some, inconstancy; in some, too much love of the world. A saint in this life is like gold in the ore: much dross of weakness clings to him, yet we love him for the grace that is in him. A saint is like a fair face with a scar: we love the beautiful face of holiness, though there is a scar on it. The best emerald has its flaws, the brightest stars their twinkling, and the best of the saints their failings. You who cannot love another because of his weaknesses, how would you have God love you? Do you not have plenty of your own?]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. We love the saints though they differ from us in some lesser things. Perhaps another Christian does not have as much light as you, and that may make him err in some things. Will you at once unsaint him because he cannot come up to your light?#footnote[☜] Where there is union in #emph[fundamentals,] there ought to be union in #emph[affections.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. We love the saints though they are persecuted. We love precious metal though it is in the furnace. Paul bore in his body the #emph[marks] of the Lord Jesus (Gal 6:17). Those marks were like the #emph[soldier’s scar,] honorable. We must love a saint in chains as well as in scarlet. #emph[Constantine] kissed the empty socket of #emph[Paphnutius’] eye, because he had lost his eye for Christ. If we love Christ, we love his persecuted members.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[If this is loving God, to love his image sparkling in the saints, oh, then how few lovers of God are to be found! Do they love God who hate those who are like God? Do they love Christ’s person who are filled with a spirit of revenge against his people? How can a wife be said to love her husband when she tears up his picture? Surely #emph[Judas] and #emph[Julian] are not dead yet; their spirit lives in the world. Who are guilty but the innocent? What greater crime is there than holiness, if the devil may sit on the grand jury? Wicked men seem to show great reverence to the saints who have died. They canonize dead saints, but persecute living ones. In vain do men stand up at the Creed and tell the world that they “believe in God,” when they loathe one of the articles of the Creed, namely, “the communion of saints.” Surely there is no greater sign that a man is ripe for hell than this: not only to #emph[lack] grace, but to #emph[hate] it.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[10\. Another blessed sign of love is #emph[to have good thoughts of God.] He who loves his friend takes what his friend does in the best sense. 1 Cor 13:5: “Love thinks no evil.” Malice interprets everything in the #emph[worst sense;] love interprets everything in the #emph[best sense.] It is an excellent commentator on providence; “it thinks no evil.” He who loves God has a good opinion of God. Though God afflicts sharply, the soul takes it all well. This is the language of a gracious spirit: “My God sees what a hard heart I have, and so he drives in one wedge of affliction after another to break my heart. He knows how full I am of bad humors, how sick with pleurisy, and so he bleeds me to save my life. This severe dealing is either to mortify some corruption or to exercise some grace. How good God is, that he will not leave me alone in my sins, but strikes my body to save my soul!” So he who loves God takes everything in good part. Love puts a fair interpretation on all God’s actions. You who are inclined to murmur against God, as if he had dealt badly with you, be humbled for this. Say to yourself, “If I loved God more, I would have better thoughts of God.” It is Satan who makes us have good thoughts of ourselves and hard thoughts of God. Love takes everything in the fairest sense; “it thinks no evil.”]

11\. The eleventh fruit of love is #emph[obedience] (John 14:21): “Whoever has My commandments and keeps them#footnote[11 #emph[Fruit.]] is the one who loves Me.” #emph[Pater adsum, impera quid vis]#footnote[#emph[Plautus.]] \[Father, here I am; command what you will\]. It is a vain thing to say we love Christ’s person if we slight his commands. Does that child love his father who refuses to obey him#footnote[#emph[Amore Deum. & obedire, duae sunt alae, quibus evoles ad coelos, transveharis ad sanctos, inseraris inter Angelos.] \[To love God and to obey are two wings, by which you may fly up to heaven, be carried over to the saints, and be set among the angels.\] Augustine, On Visiting the Sick.]? If we love God, we will obey him in those things that cross flesh and blood:

#[
#set enum(indent: 24pt)
+ In things that are difficult.
+ In things that are dangerous.
]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. In #emph[things that are difficult.] As: (1) In mortifying sin. There are some sins that are not only near to us as our clothes, but dear to us as our eye. If we love God, we will set ourselves against these, both in purpose and in practice. (2) In forgiving our enemies. God commands us on pain of death to forgive (Eph 4:32): “forgiving each other”#footnote[πρὸς τὸν ἐχθρὸν μηδέποτε ἀπεχθῶς ἔχε. \[Never bear yourself with hatred toward your enemy.\]]. This is hard; it is going against the stream. We are apt to forget kindnesses and remember injuries. But if we love God, we will pass over offenses. When we seriously consider how many talents God has forgiven us, and how many affronts and insults he has put up with from us, it makes us copy his example and try to bury an injury rather than pay it back.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. In #emph[things that are dangerous.] When God calls us to suffer for him, we will obey. Love made Christ suffer for us; love was the chain that fastened him to the cross. So if we love God, we will be willing to suffer for him. Love has a strange quality: it is the least suffering grace, and yet it is the most suffering grace. It is the least suffering grace in one sense: it will not suffer known sin to lie in the soul unrepented of, and it will not suffer abuses and dishonors to be done to God. In this way it is the least suffering grace. Yet it is the most suffering grace: it will suffer reproaches, chains, and imprisonment for Christ’s sake (Acts 21:13): “I am ready not only to be bound, but also to die … for the name of the Lord Jesus.” It is true that not every Christian is a martyr, but he has a spirit of martyrdom in him. He says with #emph[Paul,] “ἑτοίμως ἔχω, I am ready … to be bound.” He has a disposition of mind to suffer, if God calls. Love will carry men beyond their own strength. #emph[Tertullian] observes how much the heathen suffered for love of their country: #emph[Vicit amor patriae] \[love of country won\]. If the spring of nature rises so high, surely grace will rise higher. If love of their country will make men suffer, much more should love to Christ. 1 Cor 13:7: “Love … endures all things.” #emph[Basil] tells of a young woman condemned to the fire. She was offered her life and her property if she would bow down to the idol, and she answered, “Let life and money go; welcome Christ”#footnote[#emph[Valeat vita, pereat pecunia.]]. It was a noble and zealous saying of #emph[Ignatius:] “Let me be ground by the teeth of wild beasts, if only I may be God’s pure wheat”#footnote[δι’ ὀδόντων θηρίων ἀλήθομαι ἵνα καθαρὸς ἄρτος θεοῦ εὑρεθῶ. Ignatius.]. How divine affection carried the first saints above the love of life and the fear of death! #emph[Stephen] was stoned, #emph[Luke] hanged on an olive tree, #emph[Peter] crucified at #emph[Jerusalem] with his head downward#footnote[#emph[Eusebius.]]. These divine #emph[heroes] were willing to suffer rather than make the name of God suffer by their cowardice. How Paul rattled the chain he wore for Christ#footnote[Acts 28:20.]! He gloried in it ὡς γύνη φιλόκοσμος, like a woman who is proud of her jewels, says #emph[Chrysostom.] And holy #emph[Ignatius] wore his fetters like a bracelet of diamonds. Heb 11:35: “Others … refused their release.” They refused to come out of prison on sinful terms; they preferred their innocence to their liberty.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Let us test our love to God by this: do we have a spirit of martyrdom? Many say they love God, but how does it show? They will not give up the least comfort or bear the least cross for his sake. Suppose Jesus Christ had said to us, “I love you#super[+] well, you#super[+] are dear to me, but I cannot suffer; I cannot lay down my life for you#super[+].” We would have questioned his love very much. And may not Christ suspect us, when we profess love to him and yet will endure nothing for him? Those who bear true affection to God will, according to their vow in baptism, fight under his banner to the death, and make Christ’s crown flourish in their ashes.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[12\. He who loves God #emph[will try to make him appear glorious in the eyes of others.]#footnote[12 #emph[Fruit.]] Those who are in love will be praising and setting forth the loveliness of the persons they love. The love-sick bride describes Christ in all his beauty (Song 5:11, 13): “His head is purest gold … His cheeks are like beds of spice.” If we love God, we will spread abroad his excellencies, so that we may raise his fame and esteem and draw others to fall in love with him. Love cannot be silent. We will be so many trumpets, sounding out the freeness of God’s grace, the surpassing greatness of his love, and the glory of his kingdom. Love is like fire: where it burns in the heart, it will break out at the lips. It will be eloquent and moving in setting forth God’s praise. Love must have a vent.]

13\. The thirteenth fruit of love is#footnote[13 #emph[Fruit.]] #emph[to long for Christ’s appearing.] 2 Tim 4:8: “From now on there is laid up for me the crown of righteousness … and not only to me, but to all who love His appearing.” Love desires union, and #emph[Aristotle] gives the reason: because joy flows from union. When our union with Christ is perfect in glory, then our joy will be full. He who loves Christ loves his appearing. Christ’s appearing will be a happy appearing for the saints. His appearing #emph[now,] when he appears for us as an advocate (Heb 9:24), is a great comfort. But the other appearing will be infinitely more, when he appears for us as our husband. On that day he will give us two jewels. (1) His love: a love so great and astonishing that it is better felt than expressed. (2) His likeness (1 John 3:2): “When Christ appears, we will be like Him.” And from both of these, #emph[love] and #emph[likeness,] infinite joy will flow into the soul. No wonder, then, that he who loves Christ longs for his appearing. Does not the bride long for the wedding day? “The Spirit and the bride say, ‘Come!’ … Come, Lord Jesus!” (Rev 22:17). Let us test our love to Christ by this. A wicked man, who is αὐτοκατάκριτος, self-condemned, is afraid of Christ’s appearing; he wishes he would never appear. But those who love Christ are joyful to think of his coming in the clouds. Then they will be delivered from all their sins and fears; they will be acquitted before men and angels, and will be carried forever into the paradise of God.

14\. #emph[Love will make us stoop to the lowliest tasks.]#footnote[14 #emph[Fruit.]] Love is a humble grace. It does not walk abroad in state; it will creep on its hands. It will stoop and submit to anything by which it may be of service to Christ. We see this in #emph[Joseph of Arimathea] and #emph[Nicodemus.] Both of them were men of honor, yet one takes down Christ’s body with his own hands, and the other embalms it with sweet spices. It might seem a great deal for persons of their rank to be employed in that service, but love made them do it. If we love God, we will not think any work too lowly for us in which we may be of help to Christ’s members. Love is not squeamish. It will visit the sick, relieve the poor, and wash the saints’ wounds. The mother who loves her child is not fussy or dainty; she will do things for her child that others would scorn to do. He who loves God will humble himself to the lowliest task of love to Christ and his members. These are the fruits of love to God. Happy are those who can find these fruits of another country growing in their souls.
